                        Patch Metadata - Gen 11/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ hex_lattice_lp_radii                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the naive ring layout with a hexagonal (triangular) lattice arrangement of 26     │
│                          │ sites, which is the densest local arrangement for equal circles and yields near-uniform   │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor distances. Then, instead of the crude pairwise scaling (which shrinks    │
│                          │ radii far too aggressively and yields only ~0.96), compute the optimal radii for the      │
│                          │ fixed centers exactly via linear programming (scipy.optimize.linprog): maximize sum(r_i)  │
│                          │ subject to r_i + r_j <= d(i,j) for all pairs and r_i <= border distance. This LP is the   │
│                          │ exact optimum for fixed centers, so any improvement comes purely from better site         │
│                          │ placement. The hexagonal layers (rows of 5,4,5,4,5,3) fill the square efficiently and     │
│                          │ place many circles near the boundary where border constraints dominate, which is          │
