│ patch_description        │ Instead of a single central circle with two rings (which wastes corner space), I build a  │
│                          │ multi-scale explicit skeleton: 4 corner circles, an asymmetric perimeter chain (3 circles │
│                          │ on the bottom edge, 2 on the others — breaking symmetry helps edge utilization), and a    │
│                          │ staggered 5-4-5-4 interior hex lattice. Radii are then computed by a Gauss-Seidel         │
│                          │ min-relaxation fixed point (r_i = min(border, min_j(d_ij - r_j))), which guarantees a     │
│                          │ valid packing and naturally produces the size grading the skeleton implies (larger        │
│                          │ corner/edge circles, smaller interior ones — matching the insight that varied radii beat  │
│                          │ uniform patterns near square boundaries). Finally, a short deterministic                  │
│                          │ "pressure-release" polish loop nudges centers away from tight contacts (overlaps and wall │
│                          │ pressures) with decaying step size, re-relaxing radii each step and keeping the best      │
│                          │ valid configuration. This combines explicit constructor structure with a lightweight      │
│                          │ geometric refinement rather than a blind search.                                          │
