│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ hex_lattice_layout_with_lp_candidates                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ The current layout uses uniform vertical spacing 1/7 ≈ 0.143, which is far too small for  │
│                          │ a hexagonal lattice: adjacent rows force pairs of radii to sum to ≤ 0.143, capping every  │
│                          │ circle near 0.07. The fix is to place circles on a true triangular (hexagonal) lattice    │
│                          │ with the correct spacing g = s·√3/2 between rows and s between columns, with margins r =  │
│                          │ s/2 to the square's borders. For 6 rows this gives s = 1/((5·√3/2)+1) ≈ 0.1875, so the    │
│                          │ base equal radius is ≈ 0.094, already yielding sum ≈ 2.4+ (vs 2.02 currently), and the LP │
│                          │ for optimal radii at fixed centers squeezes out more by letting edge/corner circles and   │
│                          │ sparser rows (e.g., the 3-circle row) grow beyond the base radius.                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I also generate several candidate row patterns (5-4-5-4-5-3, 4-5-4-5-4-4, and a variant   │
│                          │ with a widened 3-circle top/bottom row) and slight lattice scalings, solve the LP for     │
│                          │ each, and return the best — this makes the constructor robust to which staggered pattern  │
│                          │ fits the square best, at negligible cost (each LP is tiny: 26 variables, ~350             │
│                          │ constraints). Staggered rows of 4 are centered so they nest into the gaps of the 5-rows   │
│                          │ at exactly the hex distance s, keeping all contacts at the ideal equilateral-triangle     │
│                          │ geometry that characterizes optimal packings.                                             │
