│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ slack_directed_lp_search                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The current program regressed badly (2.37 → 1.69) for two reasons: (1) `fast_radii` is a  │
│                          │ monotone shrinking heuristic that systematically *underestimates* the optimal radii, so   │
│                          │ the hill-climb accepts/rejects moves based on a biased objective, wasting the whole       │
│                          │ search budget on noise; (2) the search spreads 4000 steps over 5 layouts with an          │
│                          │ isotropic random walk that has low acceptance probability. The earlier LP-based version   │
│                          │ proved the LP objective is what makes the search work (2.37 in only 250 steps).           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The fix: restore the exact LP as the objective inside the loop, cut the step count to fit │
│                          │ the time budget, and — following the targeted-move recommendation — replace isotropic     │
│                          │ Gaussian jitter with *slack-directed* moves: for each circle compute its slack (distance  │
│                          │ to nearest wall minus radius, and gaps to touching neighbors) and perturb preferentially  │
│                          │ along the direction of maximum slack, so a large fraction of the step lies in directions  │
│                          │ that are feasible by construction and thus more likely to be accepted. Random jitter is   │
│                          │ kept at a small fraction to preserve exploration. Fewer, more informative steps (each     │
│                          │ with exact LP evaluation) plus fewer layouts keeps runtime around the earlier ~1.5–3 s    │
│                          │ range while improving the attained sum.                                                   │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
