│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ hex_grid_packing_26                                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the naive ring layout with a hexagonal-grid-based arrangement. For n=26 in a unit │
│                          │ square, an optimal-style packing uses rows of circles with staggered (hexagonal) offsets  │
│                          │ and roughly equal radii. A known good structure is rows with counts 6,5,6,5,4 (26         │
│                          │ circles), or 5,6,5,6,4. Place rows at equal vertical spacing with hexagonal stagger, then │
│                          │ compute radii via a proper iterative water-filling: each circle's radius is limited by    │
│                          │ half the distance to its nearest neighbor and by distance to the walls, then repeatedly   │
│                          │ shrink to satisfy pairwise constraints (r_i + r_j ≤ d_ij). Using equal radii r ≈          │
│                          │ 1/(2*5.5) ≈ 0.0909 in staggered rows gives sum ≈ 26*0.09 ≈ 2.36+, far above the current   │
│                          │ 0.96. The iterative radii solver converging on a min of (wall limit, neighbor             │
│                          │ constraints) is more correct than the one-shot proportional scaling.                      │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0067                                                                                   │
