│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ slack_directed_hillclimb                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The best prior program (sum 2.40) achieved its score via a randomized hill-climb over     │
│                          │ center positions with LP re-solves; the current program dropped that search, losing       │
│                          │ performance (2.36). I restore the hill-climb but improve it per the recommendation:       │
│                          │ instead of isotropic Gaussian steps, each move perturbs a circle along its "slack"        │
│                          │ direction — the direction of maximum available space (away from its nearest blocking wall │
│                          │ or neighbor) — blended with a small random component. This raises acceptance probability  │
│                          │ of each LP re-solve, so the fixed time budget (~1.2s) yields more improvements. I also    │
│                          │ bias circle selection toward circles with small radii (they have the most slack to        │
│                          │ exploit). A lightweight non-LP score proxy (minimum pairwise/wall slack) lets us reject   │
│                          │ clearly-bad moves before paying the LP cost, further increasing effective throughput.     │
