│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multistart_lprefine_hillclimb                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current program fixes centers via a single hand-designed hex layout and solves radii  │
│                          │ exactly with LP, reaching sum=2.02. The new program keeps the exact LP radii solver       │
│                          │ (vectorized for speed) but makes it a subroutine inside a two-phase optimizer: (1)        │
│                          │ generate ~10 diverse base layouts (hex row patterns with several spacings/offsets plus    │
│                          │ jittered variants), score each cheaply via LP; (2) spend the remaining time budget (~2.0s │
│                          │ deadline) doing adaptive-step random-restart hill climbing on the winning layout's        │
│                          │ centers — each move perturbs one circle center, and moves are accepted only if the LP sum │
│                          │ increases; the step size anneals and resets on improvement plateaus. This exploits the    │
│                          │ fact that sum-of-radii is exactly the LP optimum given centers, so center moves directly  │
│                          │ optimize the objective. Time-based deadline avoids overrunning the eval budget.           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
