│ patch_name               │ jitter_multistart_adaptive_anneal                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ Expert insight: for n=26 in a unit square, the optimal packing is close to a hexagonal    │
│                          │ (triangular) lattice with defect rows near the walls, so the hex-rows seed is by far the  │
│                          │ strongest basin — but starting all four runs from exact lattice positions makes the       │
│                          │ annealer rediscover the same trajectory. Following the recommendation, I (1) jitter each  │
│                          │ start's centers with a small Gaussian perturbation (different seed per start) to          │
│                          │ decorrelate basins while keeping the strong base layout, (2) bias the annealer's moves    │
│                          │ toward circles that are "tight" (small radii / active constraints) since those have the   │
│                          │ most slack to gain, and (3) shrink the per-start step budget gracefully and give the hex  │
│                          │ seed extra steps. This directly extends the mechanism that produced the best score this   │
│                          │ round at negligible runtime cost.                                                         │
