│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ sparse_lp_multistart_prescreen                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ The current program regressed (2.38) because it uses the slow dense-matrix LP assembly    │
│                          │ (O(n²) Python loops building a dense A_ub) and only a single-start hill climb that tracks │
│                          │ only the current state, so a temporarily bad accepted move can erase progress. The best   │
│                          │ prior program (2.41, score 0.92) combined the vectorized sparse-CSR LP with a multi-start │
│                          │ slack-directed climb and best-ever tracking. I restore that combination and add the       │
│                          │ recommended cheap geometric pre-screen: before each exact LP call, evaluate the greedy    │
│                          │ `compute_max_radii` sum (microseconds for n=26); if it falls more than 0.01 below the     │
│                          │ current basin's sum, reject the trial without solving the LP, since greedy rarely         │
│                          │ underestimates the LP optimum by more than that margin for small perturbations. This      │
│                          │ roughly halves the LP calls per iteration, letting each start basin perform many more     │
│                          │ moves within the same ~1.9s budget, while the row-shift/block-shift collective moves are  │
│                          │ retained for better exploration of lattice-type packings.                                 │
