                        Patch Metadata - Gen 45/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_collective_row_and_cluster_moves                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ All prior variants that perturb only 1–3 individual circles plateau at sum ≈ 2.36–2.41.   │
│                          │ This is characteristic of lattice-aligned local optima: the seed is a triangular lattice, │
│                          │ and moving single circles off their lattice sites almost always decreases the LP total,   │
│                          │ so the hill-climb gets stuck even though sliding whole rigid rows/clusters to better      │
│                          │ global positions could gain. The fix is to add two collective move types to the           │
│                          │ hill-climb, alongside the existing single-circle moves:                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Row-shift (≈10% of moves):** pick an anchor circle, gather all centers within 0.02   │
│                          │ in y (one hex row, since row spacing is ≈0.16), and translate that whole row horizontally │
│                          │ by ±0.005–0.02. This slides a rigid triangular-lattice row against its neighbors without  │
│                          │ breaking the local equilateral geometry, letting rows redistribute slack from the         │
│                          │ square's vertical mirror symmetry.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Cluster-shift (≈10% of moves):** pick an anchor, gather all centers within 0.25 of   │
│                          │ it, and translate that coherent cluster by a common small random delta. This moves rigid  │
│                          │ hex patches (e.g., the 5-4-5 core) as a unit toward the walls or into underused corners,  │
│                          │ which per-circle jitter can never accomplish.                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Both move types preserve the local pairwise distances inside the moved group, so the LP   │
│                          │ (which is exact given centers) re-evaluates only the group's interaction with the rest of │
│                          │ the square — collective moves explore a direction in configuration space that             │
│                          │ single-circle moves cannot reach. The remaining 80% of moves stay single-circle to        │
│                          │ fine-tune individual sites. I also extend the time budget from 0.9s to 1.8s (prior runs   │
│                          │ completed within 2.7s comfortably) and grow the step slightly on successful moves so      │
│                          │ accepted collective shifts can be amplified.                                              │
