│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ sparse_lp_anneal_pack                                                                     │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of rebuilding a dense (n²+n)-row constraint matrix with Python loops per          │
│                          │ evaluation (the dominant cost in all prior versions), I precompute the static pair/wall   │
│                          │ index arrays once and assemble the LP as a sparse COO matrix on each call — only the      │
│                          │ distances change between evaluations. This cuts per-evaluation cost by ~10x, letting the  │
│                          │ search run many more moves. On top of that I run a multi-basin simulated annealing with   │
│                          │ slack-directed moves and cheap fast_radii pre-screening (only pay LP cost when the        │
│                          │ screening proxy looks promising), plus a final exact LP polish. This directly implements  │
│                          │ the vectorized-LP recommendation while keeping the slack-directed hill-climb philosophy   │
│                          │ of the context programs.                                                                  │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
