│ patch_description        │ The recommendation targets the key weakness observed across generations: all search       │
│                          │ effort concentrates on a single basin (the best lattice layout explored by one SA         │
│                          │ trajectory), and longer runtimes did not translate into better radii (the 2.56s           │
│                          │ multi-start run scored no better than the 0.56s constructor, while the short 0.9s greedy  │
│                          │ climb reached 2.40 — the best so far). Combined score also rewards low eval_time, so the  │
│                          │ budget should be modest (~1.4s), not 2.2–2.7s.                                            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Changes:                                                                                  │
│                          │ 1. Enrich the seeding pass with scale variants (0.98, 1.0) so the "best basin" is chosen  │
│                          │ from a wider lattice family — more diverse basins to jitter around.                       │
│                          │ 2. Replace the simulated annealing block with the recommended multi-start jittered greedy │
│                          │ hill-climb: each of 4 starts perturbs the winning lattice with its own ±0.01 Gaussian     │
│                          │ jitter (distinct seed per start, decorrelating the basins per the Gen-8 lesson), uses the │
│                          │ proven adaptive-step move set (1–3 circles per move, shrink-when-stuck /                  │
│                          │ occasional-grow), and receives a shrinking per-start time share `(deadline −              │
│                          │ now)/(n_starts − start)` so early starts explore broadly while the last start does fine   │
│                          │ polish. Total budget is capped at 1.4s to keep eval_time near the regime that scored      │
│                          │ 0.91, while the multi-start jitter gives more independent chances to escape local optima  │
│                          │ that a single greedy or SA trajectory cannot.                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This directly combines the two mechanisms that individually produced the best results     │
│                          │ (multi-start jitter diversity from program 2, short-budget adaptive greedy from program   │
│                          │ 4) while avoiding the time-bloat that hurt programs 2 and 3.                              │
