│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ interior_point_grad_descent_packing                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ Fundamentally different approach: instead of hill-climbing discrete center positions with │
│                          │ an LP radius solver, treat the packing as a continuous optimization problem with radii    │
│                          │ and centers as joint decision variables. Maximize sum(r) + penalty for overlaps and wall  │
│                          │ violations using a penalty method with L-BFGS-B gradient descent. The penalty P = Σ       │
│                          │ relu(r_i + r_j - d_ij)² + Σ relu(r_i - wall_i)² has simple analytic gradients w.r.t. both │
│                          │ centers and radii. Anneal the penalty weight high→low so the solution converges to a      │
│                          │ feasible packing while pushing radii up. Use a good hex seed plus random restarts with    │
│                          │ jitter. This should outperform hill-climbing because it can move all 52 variables         │
│                          │ simultaneously along the true objective gradient, escaping the coordinate-wise            │
│                          │ limitations, and it's fast (each L-BFGS-B run is milliseconds).                           │
