                        Patch Metadata - Gen 48/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ expand_seeds_multistart_climb                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ The current seed screen only tests 8 row patterns, and the hill-climb then commits all of │
│                          │ its 1.6s budget to a single basin of attraction — the best seed by initial LP score. Two  │
│                          │ targeted improvements:                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Denser-edge and mirrored row templates.** Gen-25's gain came from seed breadth, but  │
│                          │ the current candidate list misses boundary-heavy structures (rows of 6 at the edges with  │
│                          │ 5s inside) and vertical mirrors of the top performers. Since `hex_layout` builds rows     │
│                          │ top-down with stagger alignment tied to row order, a vertically mirrored pattern is       │
│                          │ geometrically distinct and can land in a different (better) LP basin. Each new template   │
│                          │ costs microseconds in the LP screen. All new row counts sum to exactly 26.                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Multi-start hill climb.** Instead of climbing only from the single best seed, run    │
│                          │ the slack-directed climb from the top 3 seeds, splitting the time budget evenly, and keep │
│                          │ the global best. The seed with the best *initial* LP value is not necessarily the seed    │
│                          │ with the best *nearby local optimum* — edge-heavy templates in particular often start     │
│                          │ lower but have more room to expand against the walls. Tracking `gbest` also guarantees we │
